tration can promote dehydration. Methacrolein is volatile and readily undergoes further reactions; retention and rapid downstream consumption are necessary rather than assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate mineral-mediated hydrogenation of methacrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"exposed native nickel or awaruite surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native nickel or awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, hydrogen-rich rock-water interface; pH approximately 7-9; cooling or removal of product limits further reduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-activated hydrogen adds across the alkene while the aldehyde group is retained.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Metal-catalyzed alkene hydrogenation is chemically appropriate, and nickel-bearing reduced phases are plausible in serpentinizing systems. This particular mineral implementation is speculative: carbonyl reduction to isobutanol, hydrogenolysis and catalyst passivation may prevent useful aldehyde selectivity. Molecular hydrogen alone is not treated as an effective catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate dehydrogenation of supplied isobutanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"exposed native copper surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-470 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Low-hydrogen-activity flowing compartment; pH approximately 6-8; physically separate from hydrogen-rich reduction zones.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": 