{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:52:51.737218", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.62\n- Feedback: The network is structurally substantial and connected: all 26 molecule nodes and 19 reaction nodes participate, and the ten pathway descriptions map consistently onto the reaction graph. The deterministic check establishes zero conservation violations. The small inferred coefficients should be made explicit: rxn_013 uses 2 H2O; rxn_017 uses 2 H+; rxn_019 uses 2 Fe, 4 H+ and produces 2 Fe2+. Resolve the reported mol_023 formula/InChI discrepancy with an unambiguous Fe(II) representation; this is not grounds for overriding the supplied balance results. The strongest chemistry is the aldehyde-to-valine Strecker sequence, with the hydantoin sequence also plausible under suitable conditions. The principal network-level limitations are unvalidated selective mineral hydrogenation in rxn_003, alcohol dehydrogenation in rxn_004, and especially alpha-hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation in rxn_014. Every proposed route depends on at least one incompletely established precursor-generating operation. Acknowledging these uncertainties is appropriate but does not experimentally validate them. Separate reaction episodes are acceptable; environmental scores reflect missing catalyst identities, temperature, pH, pressure, hydrogen activity, residence times and physically specified transfer/retention mechanisms, not the number or order of environments. Boundary feedstocks are accepted as stipulated, and racemic valine satisfies the target. No research findings were supplied, so literature alignment is not separately scored. Overall, this is a coherent conditional research network with significant implementation gaps, not a demonstrated complete synthesis, and it does not meet the 0.9 network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest conditional route. The carbon skeleton construction and Strecker sequence are chemically coherent, and aminonitrile hydrolysis can yield racemic valine. The principal implementation gap is selective mineral-mediated hydrogenation of methacrolein: neither the active mineral phase nor conditions establishing aldehyde selectivity are supplied. Surface selectivity, hydrolysis conditions and intermediate retention also remain unquantified.', 'The downstream Strecker chemistry is credible, but production of the required aldehyde from supplied isobutanol is not established under the stated hydrothermal implementation. Acceptorless alcohol dehydrogenation requires suitable catalysis and favorable hydrogen activity. Permitting a separate low-hydrogen episode avoids an automatic environmental contradiction, but does not establish useful conversion or selectivity.', 'The aminonitrile-to-hydantoin transformation is a plausible Bucherer-Bergs net reaction, and the identified hydrolysis intermediates lead correctly to valine. However, hydantoin ring opening and carbamoyl removal cannot be assumed from a hydrothermal label alone; they require appropriate reaction severity and speciation. The selective methacrolein hydrogenation remains an upstream uncertainty.', 'This route has a valid formal connection from isobutanol to racemic valine, but its aldehyde supply depends on unvalidated mineral-mediated dehydrogenation. The hydantoin branch is recognizable chemistry rather than a demonstrated geochemical sequence. Both hydantoin opening and subsequent deprotection need condition-specific evidence, particularly for useful rates without substantial product loss.', 'The carbon skeleton and intermediates are correctly connected, but alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid dehydrogenation is a major unsupported step. No demonstrated catalyst, favorable equilibrium window or useful keto-acid yield is supplied. Hydrogen-driven imino-acid reduction also requires an identified active catalyst. Separate oxidative and reductive episodes are allowed, but their operation and intermediate survival remain unverified.', 'Two required substrate-generating operations remain unvalidated: isobutanol dehydrogenation and alpha-hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation. The final hydrogen-dependent reduction is also catalyst-dependent. The sequence is formally sensible, but identifying separate low- and high-hydrogen episodes does not establish their thermodynamics, kinetics or achievable valine yield.', 'Native iron provides a chemically credible two-electron reductant for imino-acid reduction, making the final reduction more concretely specified than an unspecified hydrogenation catalyst. Nevertheless, the pathway still depends on unsupported alpha-hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation. Iron passivation, competing hydrogen evolution, ammonia speciation and transient imine availability must be addressed experimentally.', 'The iron-driven final reduction is chemically plausible, but it cannot compensate for unestablished production of both isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid. The native iron starting node is accepted as supplied; the uncertainty concerns its reactivity and persistence under the eventual solution conditions, not its inclusion as a feedstock.', 'Oxime formation from the keto acid and supplied hydroxylamine is sound chemistry. Iron reduction can in principle deliver the required four electrons for conversion to valine, but substrate-specific selectivity and reaction conditions are not demonstrated. The more fundamental limitation remains production of the keto acid through rxn_014.', 'The oxime branch is formally valid and uses a plausible final reduction class, but the complete pathway depends on two unvalidated dehydrogenations. Supplied hydroxylamine is an acceptable boundary reagent and is not itself a scoring defect. Useful valine production remains conditional on establishing precursor generation and selective iron-mediated oxime reduction.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Produce racemic valine through ten graph-distinct pathways: six combine two aminonitrile-entry sequences with three downstream processing options, and four begin with 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid. The strongest core is conventional aldehyde-based Strecker chemistry. Isobutyraldehyde and the branched keto acid are explicitly supplied boundary feedstocks; their geochemical production is not claimed. This removes the previously unsupported selective hydrogenation and alcohol/hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation steps. Hydroxylamine and amino-acid nitrogen donors are also conditional boundary inputs. These are ten alternative reaction histories, not ten independent mechanisms or ten experimentally demonstrated geochemical syntheses. Sulfur-assisted processing, severe hydantoin hydrolysis, iron-mediated reductions, and metal-promoted transaminations retain the limitations identified in their reasoning fields. Conditions are proposed experimental windows, not reported optimized protocols. Surface precursor pools and hot processing compartments are separate anoxic episodes connected by porewater flow, spillover, cooling, and mixing; no simultaneous incompatible conditions are assumed. Organic formulas use neutral reference states, with acid-base speciation implicit except for explicitly balanced metal-redox protons. Fe+2 denotes one iron atom with charge +2. Listed pH values are room-temperature reference measurements; hot-fluid speciation requires recalculation. No molecular oxygen, enzymes, or stereoselective catalyst is required.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine thioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-Isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-Carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-imino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-iminoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Native iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proton\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Aspartic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H7NO4/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h2H,1,5H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in covered or sediment-confined pores\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.0; ammonia in excess over aldehyde; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Shaded, low-exchange pores limit photolysis and volatile losses. The imine remains an equilibrium intermediate and is not assumed to accumulate as an isolated feed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to the aldimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the established carbonyl-condensation entry to Strecker chemistry. Water favors competing hydrolysis, so useful flux depends on subsequent cyanide trapping rather than an assumed high isolated imine yield. Supplying isobutyraldehyde directly avoids an unsupported precursor-generating hydrogenation or dehydrogenation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide trapping of the aldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in confined porewater\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.0; proposed total cyanide 1-50 mM and residence 1-48 hours. Operates in the same reacting solution as rxn_001. Keep this compartment poor in dissolved transition metals that would sequester cyanide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conventional Strecker carbon-carbon bond formation supplies the future carboxyl carbon and gives the correct branched C5 skeleton. Attack on an achiral precursor produces racemic material. Cyanide availability, competing cyanohydrin formation, and precursor volatilization constrain yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of the aldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by mildly alkaline porewater\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in covered pores\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide as a general-base promoter\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.0; proposed residence 0.5-24 hours. Initially lower ammonia activity than in rxn_001 favors observing the cyanohydrin-containing reservoir. Retain dissolved HCN and aldehyde by limited gas exchange.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and the resulting alkoxide is protonated.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is a well-established competing reaction of aldehydes with HCN. Including this species is chemically justified by the actual precursor mixture, rather than by a need to manufacture graph connectivity. Its abundance is equilibrium-dependent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of a cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in confined porewater\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.5-9.5; ammonia-rich inflow mixes with the cyanohydrin-containing pool; proposed residence 6-72 hours. Maintain limited gas exchange rather than requiring isolation or drying of the cyanohydrin.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net conversion through cyanohydrin dissociation, aldehyde-ammonia equilibration, and cyanide trapping of the resulting imine. This is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin-containing mixtures can feed Strecker aminonitrile formation through their reversible carbonyl chemistry. This creates a different precursor-storage and reaction history, but not an independent elementary mechanism from rxn_001 and rxn_002. Its usefulness depends on retention and redistribution of the shared aldehyde/cyanide pool.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton supplied by chloride-rich acidic geothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 0.5-1.5; proposed residence 0.5-24 hours. Aminonitrile-bearing water enters an acidic geothermal fracture after leaving the cyanide-formation compartment. Valinamide may remain transient and need not be isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition, and imidic-acid tautomerization give the primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-amide hydration is established chemistry and supplies the first hydrolysis stage of the Strecker product. Acidic conditions suppress some aminonitrile condensation reactions. Actual residence time must be established against decomposition and further amide hydrolysis; a hydrothermal setting alone does not establish conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valinamide to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton supplied by acidic geothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 0.5-1.5; proposed additional residence 6-72 hours. Continue the acidic aqueous episode, then cool the effluent below 320 K. Released nitrogen is predominantly ammonium at operating pH, represented by its neutral NH3 reference state.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage yields valine and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the chemically strongest target-forming sequence. Together, rxn_005 and rxn_006 consume two water molecules per aminonitrile. The amide hydrolysis can be slower than initial nitrile hydration, making measured valine recovery after heating and quenching essential.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in covered sulfidic pores\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.5; proposed total dissolved sulfide 10-100 mM and residence 1-7 days. HS- is the reactive acid-base form of the H2S input. Use a sulfidic compartment separate from the iron-reduction and cyanide-storage compartments.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers and tautomerization produce an alpha-amino thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conversion of nitriles to thioamides by sulfide is established reaction-class chemistry and offers a chemically motivated sulfur-mediated alternative. Useful accumulation of this particular alpha-amino thioamide under the stated geochemical conditions is not established here. Cyclization, condensation, and competing hydrolysis must be measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic sulfur-oxygen exchange in valine thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton from acidic geothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-415 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 0.5-2.0; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Transfer thioamide-bearing porewater into a separate acidic hot zone. Advective removal and downstream degassing limit sulfide accumulation; continued processing can feed rxn_006 without amide isolation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of the thiocarbonyl and sulfur elimination replace sulfur with oxygen, giving valinamide and H2S.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thioamide-to-amide hydrolysis is chemically plausible and regenerates sulfide in the net sulfur-assisted hydration sequence. However, selective passage through valinamide for this substrate under these conditions is a proposed implementation, not a demonstrated prebiotic result. This branch must outperform competing decomposition to justify its use over direct nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon dioxide incorporation and hydantoin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ammonia/ammonium general-acid-base buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar with restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ammonia/ammonium buffer\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.5-9.5; proposed total inorganic carbon 0.05-0.5 M and residence 1-7 days. CO2 represents the incorporated inorganic-carbon reference species; bicarbonate and carbonate dominate much of the dissolved pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile N-carboxylation is followed by cyclization and rearrangement to the hydantoin. The nitrile nitrogen becomes a ring nitrogen and CO2 supplies the additional carbonyl carbon. Water-mediated proton transfers have no net water stoichiometry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the recognizable Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin branch with correct net incorporation of one CO2 per aminonitrile. It provides a chemically different downstream intermediate, but hydantoin formation does not itself demonstrate successful valine release. Inorganic-carbon activity and product solubility require measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline opening of 5-isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved hydroxide in concentrated alkaline brine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"430-460 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide; strong base also controls product speciation\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-0.5 M hydroxide and residence 1-48 hours. Requires an uncommon, strongly alkaline concentrated fluid entering confined hot pores, not an ordinary pH 9-11 vent. N-Carbamoylvaline is present primarily as its carboxylate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-promoted attack at the hydantoin carboxyl-derived carbonyl opens the ring to N-carbamoylvaline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alkaline hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but substantial severity can be required without enzymes. The unusually strong alkalinity and preservation of the product are explicit geochemical bottlenecks. These conditions are not inferred merely from the presence of a hydrothermal environment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic removal of the carbamoyl group\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton from chloride-rich acidic geothermal fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-435 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-60 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 0.5-1.0; proposed residence 12-72 hours. First cool the alkaline effluent, then mix it with a separate acidic fluid and reheat in a downstream pore zone. Cool the product promptly after treatment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the ureido group followed by carbamic-acid decomposition releases the amino group as valine, together with ammonia and CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The net transformation is chemically valid, but uncatalyzed biological-style decarbamoylation cannot be assumed. Acid-promoted hydantoin reclosure and destruction during prolonged heating compete with useful release. This is a key condition-dependent bottleneck, and the hydantoin pathways remain conditional until isolated valine recovery is demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of alpha-iminoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 6.5-8.0; proposed total ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M and keto acid 1-20 mM. Use the same flowing compartment as rxn_013 over a proposed 1-48 hour contact period; do not isolate the imino acid.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the keto group and the carbinolamine reversibly loses water, producing a low-abundance imino-acid intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional imine-forming component of reductive amination. The principal limitation is equilibrium abundance in water, especially when ammonia is mostly protonated. Coupled reduction must trap the intermediate faster than it hydrolyzes. The keto acid is a stated feedstock, not the product of an unsupported hydroxy-acid oxidation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Stoichiometric native-iron reduction of the imino acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh native-iron surface; mol_019 is a consumed reductant, not a catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 6.5-8.0; chloride-bearing, sulfide-poor and carbonate-poor water; proposed contact 1-48 hours with freshly exposed iron grains. Maintain acidity through inflow. No imposed H2 activity is needed because metallic iron supplies the electrons.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface electron transfer and protonation reduce the C=N group. Per mole of imino acid, one Fe atom and two protons are consumed, producing one valine and one Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Native iron supplies an explicit two-electron budget and avoids invoking an unidentified hydrogenation catalyst. This is a plausible reductive-amination implementation, not a guaranteed substrate-specific yield. Iron passivation, competing hydrogen evolution, reduction of unaminated keto acid, and limited imine availability remain measurable limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Weak aqueous acid for general-acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton at mildly acidic pH\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 4.5-6.5; proposed keto acid 1-20 mM, hydroxylamine near stoichiometric or modestly in excess, and residence 1-48 hours. Keep the shaded formation pool free of metallic iron until condensation has occurred.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl and loses water to form an oxime; E/Z isomers may coexist.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime formation is well-established carbonyl chemistry and can provide a more persistent nitrogen-bearing intermediate than the ammonia-derived imine. Hydroxylamine availability is a boundary assumption, not an internally demonstrated prebiotic synthesis. Photolysis, metal-induced hydroxylamine destruction, and oxime stability require control.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Four-electron native-iron reduction of the oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh native-iron surface; metallic iron is consumed stoichiometrically\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-385 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 3.0-5.0 in chloride-bearing, sulfide-poor water; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Acidic inflow sustains proton supply. Screen at least the stoichiometric iron requirement and monitor dissolved Fe2+, gas evolution, and carbon-containing side products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"N-O bond reduction and reduction of the imine functionality furnish the amino acid. Per mole of oxime, two Fe atoms and four protons are consumed, producing one valine, one water, and two Fe2+ ions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction has an explicit four-electron and charge-balanced reduction budget. Oxime-to-amine reduction by metallic reductants is established as a reaction class, but selective retention of this alpha-carboxyl group under the proposed iron-water conditions is not demonstrated here. Decarboxylation, incomplete reduction, and iron passivation make this a conditional alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron(II)-promoted transamination from alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ coordination promoter, chemically identical to mol_021\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+; catalytic turnover is not assumed without measurement\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 6.0-8.0; proposed Fe2+ 1-20 mM, keto acid 1-10 mM, and alanine 0.05-0.5 M; contact 1-7 days. Use naturally Fe(II)-bearing, sulfide-poor water with little phosphate or carbonate. No hydroxylamine, H2, or metallic iron is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-assisted Schiff-base formation, proton transfer between donor and acceptor carbon skeletons, and hydrolysis transfer the amino group from alanine to the branched keto acid, releasing pyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a redox-neutral alternative with a different nitrogen-transfer mechanism. Metal-assisted nonenzymatic transamination is chemically credible, but useful valine production from this specific donor-acceptor pair under the proposed conditions is not established here. Branched-substrate kinetics, reversibility, metal complexation, and competing keto-acid reactions must be tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Aspartate transamination coupled to oxaloacetate decarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Fe2+ as a coordination and decarboxylation promoter\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous Fe2+; productive turnover must be measured\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reference pH 6.0-7.5; proposed Fe2+ 1-20 mM, keto acid 1-10 mM, and aspartate 10-100 mM; contact 1-7 days. Use sulfide-poor Fe(II)-bearing water. Subsequent advective dilution of inorganic carbon can favor the coupled net reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aspartate transfers its amino group through a metal-assisted Schiff-base sequence, yielding valine and transient oxaloacetate. Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation converts oxaloacetate to pyruvate and CO2. This node represents the coupled net transformation, not a single elementary reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxaloacetate decarboxylation provides a chemically motivated sink for the donor-derived keto acid and distinguishes this route from simple alanine transamination. The coupled valine-forming implementation is speculative: favorable coproduct decomposition does not establish a useful rate for the preceding transamination. Direct aspartate decomposition and branched-keto-acid side reactions must be quantified.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry Strecker synthesis with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied isobutyraldehyde condenses with ammonia, is trapped by cyanide, and undergoes sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis to racemic valine. This is the strongest core route. The two hydrolysis reactions together consume two water molecules per aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir Strecker synthesis with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The aldehyde first enters a cyanohydrin reservoir. Ammonia-rich mixing redistributes this pool through reversible carbonyl chemistry into aminonitrile, followed by direct hydrolysis to valine. This differs from P1 in precursor history, not in the ultimate elementary cyanide-trapping mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry synthesis through a hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"The imine-entry aminonitrile incorporates inorganic carbon to form 5-isopropylhydantoin. Strong alkaline ring opening and a separate acidic decarbamoylation episode release valine. Severe hydrolysis conditions and intermediate survival are explicit bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir synthesis through a hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"A cyanohydrin-containing precursor pool feeds aminonitrile formation and then the hydantoin branch. The route requires both successful reservoir conversion and experimentally verified ring opening and carbamoyl removal.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry synthesis with sulfur-assisted nitrile processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The imine-entry aminonitrile reacts with sulfide to form a thioamide. Hydrolytic sulfur-oxygen exchange yields valinamide and regenerates H2S, after which amide hydrolysis gives valine. The substrate-specific sulfur-processing sequence remains conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir synthesis with sulfur-assisted processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion supplies aminonitrile to the sulfide/thioamide branch. Subsequent sulfur-oxygen exchange and amide hydrolysis yield valine if competing sulfur chemistry and decomposition remain limited.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"description\": \"The supplied branched keto acid equilibrates with ammonia to form a transient imino acid, which native iron reduces to valine. The route has an explicit stoichiometric electron donor and does not require an upstream hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxime formation followed by native-iron reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied hydroxylamine converts the branched keto acid to an oxime. Four-electron reduction by native iron can furnish valine, conditional on retaining the carboxyl group and completing both nitrogen-oxygen and carbon-nitrogen reductions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Alanine-to-valine amino-group transfer\",\n      \"description\": \"A proposed Fe(II)-promoted transamination transfers nitrogen from supplied alanine to supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate, yielding valine and pyruvate. Useful rates and equilibrium conversion for this exact substrate pair remain to be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Aspartate-driven transamination with a decarboxylating coproduct\",\n      \"description\": \"A proposed coupled Fe(II)-promoted sequence transfers nitrogen from supplied aspartate to alpha-ketoisovalerate. Decomposition of the transient oxaloacetate coproduct to pyruvate and CO2 can pull the reaction toward valine, but productive coupling requires experimental validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Produce racemic valine through ten graph-distinct pathways: six combine two aminonitrile-entry sequences with three downstream processing options, and four begin with 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid. The strongest core is conventional aldehyde-based Strecker chemistry. Isobutyraldehyde and the branched keto acid are explicitly supplied boundary feedstocks; their geochemical production is not claimed. This removes the previously unsupported selective hydrogenation and alcohol/hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation steps. Hydroxylamine and amino-acid nitrogen donors are also conditional boundary inputs. These are ten alternative reaction histories, not ten independent mechanisms or ten experimentally demonstrated geochemical syntheses. Sulfur-assisted processing, severe hydantoin hydrolysis, iron-mediated reductions, and metal-promoted transaminations retain the limitations identified in their reasoning fields. Conditions are proposed experimental windows, not reported optimized protocols. Surface precursor pools and hot processing compartments are separate anoxic episodes connected by porewater flow, spillover, cooling, and mixing; no simultaneous incompatible conditions are assumed. Organic formulas use neutral reference states, with acid-base speciation implicit except for explicitly balanced metal-redox protons. Fe+2 denotes one iron atom with charge +2. Listed pH values are room-temperature reference measurements; hot-fluid speciation requires recalculation. No molecular oxygen, enzymes, or stereoselective catalyst is required.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "Isobutyraldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H12N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanamide", "common_name": "Valinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H12N2S", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide", "common_name": "Valine thioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-Isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H12N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylvaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "2-imino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-iminoisovaleric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C4H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "Aspartic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO4/c5-2(4(8)9)1-3(6)7/h2H,1,5H2,(H,6,7)(H,8,9)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reversible formation of isobutyraldimine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in covered or sediment-confined pores", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.0; ammonia in excess over aldehyde; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Shaded, low-exchange pores limit photolysis and volatile losses. The imine remains an equilibrium intermediate and is not assumed to accumulate as an isolated feed."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to the aldimine.", "reasoning": "This is the established carbonyl-condensation entry to Strecker chemistry. Water favors competing hydrolysis, so useful flux depends on subsequent cyanide trapping rather than an assumed high isolated imine yield. Supplying isobutyraldehyde directly avoids an unsupported precursor-generating hydrogenation or dehydrogenation."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide trapping of the aldimine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in confined porewater", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.0; proposed total cyanide 1-50 mM and residence 1-48 hours. Operates in the same reacting solution as rxn_001. Keep this compartment poor in dissolved transition metals that would sequester cyanide."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Conventional Strecker carbon-carbon bond formation supplies the future carboxyl carbon and gives the correct branched C5 skeleton. Attack on an achiral precursor produces racemic material. Cyanide availability, competing cyanohydrin formation, and precursor volatilization constrain yield."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Reversible formation of the aldehyde cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by mildly alkaline porewater"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in covered pores", "catalyst": "Hydroxide as a general-base promoter", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.0; proposed residence 0.5-24 hours. Initially lower ammonia activity than in rxn_001 favors observing the cyanohydrin-containing reservoir. Retain dissolved HCN and aldehyde by limited gas exchange."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and the resulting alkoxide is protonated.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is a well-established competing reaction of aldehydes with HCN. Including this species is chemically justified by the actual precursor mixture, rather than by a need to manufacture graph connectivity. Its abundance is equilibrium-dependent."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of a cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in confined porewater", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.5-9.5; ammonia-rich inflow mixes with the cyanohydrin-containing pool; proposed residence 6-72 hours. Maintain limited gas exchange rather than requiring isolation or drying of the cyanohydrin."}, "mechanism": "Net conversion through cyanohydrin dissociation, aldehyde-ammonia equilibration, and cyanide trapping of the resulting imine. This is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin-containing mixtures can feed Strecker aminonitrile formation through their reversible carbonyl chemistry. This creates a different precursor-storage and reaction history, but not an independent elementary mechanism from rxn_001 and rxn_002. Its usefulness depends on retention and redistribution of the shared aldehyde/cyanide pool."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton supplied by chloride-rich acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-420 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 0.5-1.5; proposed residence 0.5-24 hours. Aminonitrile-bearing water enters an acidic geothermal fracture after leaving the cyanide-formation compartment. Valinamide may remain transient and need not be isolated."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and imidic-acid tautomerization give the primary amide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile-to-amide hydration is established chemistry and supplies the first hydrolysis stage of the Strecker product. Acidic conditions suppress some aminonitrile condensation reactions. Actual residence time must be established against decomposition and further amide hydrolysis; a hydrothermal setting alone does not establish conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of valinamide to valine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton supplied by acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-420 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 0.5-1.5; proposed additional residence 6-72 hours. Continue the acidic aqueous episode, then cool the effluent below 320 K. Released nitrogen is predominantly ammonium at operating pH, represented by its neutral NH3 reference state."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage yields valine and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This completes the chemically strongest target-forming sequence. Together, rxn_005 and rxn_006 consume two water molecules per aminonitrile. The amide hydrolysis can be slower than initial nitrile hydration, making measured valine recovery after heating and quenching essential."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Sulfide addition to valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "315-345 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in covered sulfidic pores", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.5; proposed total dissolved sulfide 10-100 mM and residence 1-7 days. HS- is the reactive acid-base form of the H2S input. Use a sulfidic compartment separate from the iron-reduction and cyanide-storage compartments."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers and tautomerization produce an alpha-amino thioamide.", "reasoning": "Conversion of nitriles to thioamides by sulfide is established reaction-class chemistry and offers a chemically motivated sulfur-mediated alternative. Useful accumulation of this particular alpha-amino thioamide under the stated geochemical conditions is not established here. Cyclization, condensation, and competing hydrolysis must be measured."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolytic sulfur-oxygen exchange in valine thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton from acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-415 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 0.5-2.0; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Transfer thioamide-bearing porewater into a separate acidic hot zone. Advective removal and downstream degassing limit sulfide accumulation; continued processing can feed rxn_006 without amide isolation."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of the thiocarbonyl and sulfur elimination replace sulfur with oxygen, giving valinamide and H2S.", "reasoning": "Thioamide-to-amide hydrolysis is chemically plausible and regenerates sulfide in the net sulfur-assisted hydration sequence. However, selective passage through valinamide for this substrate under these conditions is a proposed implementation, not a demonstrated prebiotic result. This branch must outperform competing decomposition to justify its use over direct nitrile hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Carbon dioxide incorporation and hydantoin formation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonia/ammonium general-acid-base buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-365 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar with restricted gas exchange", "catalyst": "Ammonia/ammonium buffer", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.5-9.5; proposed total inorganic carbon 0.05-0.5 M and residence 1-7 days. CO2 represents the incorporated inorganic-carbon reference species; bicarbonate and carbonate dominate much of the dissolved pool."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile N-carboxylation is followed by cyclization and rearrangement to the hydantoin. The nitrile nitrogen becomes a ring nitrogen and CO2 supplies the additional carbonyl carbon. Water-mediated proton transfers have no net water stoichiometry.", "reasoning": "This is the recognizable Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin branch with correct net incorporation of one CO2 per aminonitrile. It provides a chemically different downstream intermediate, but hydantoin formation does not itself demonstrate successful valine release. Inorganic-carbon activity and product solubility require measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Alkaline opening of 5-isopropylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Dissolved hydroxide in concentrated alkaline brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "430-460 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide; strong base also controls product speciation", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 0.1-0.5 M hydroxide and residence 1-48 hours. Requires an uncommon, strongly alkaline concentrated fluid entering confined hot pores, not an ordinary pH 9-11 vent. N-Carbamoylvaline is present primarily as its carboxylate."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-promoted attack at the hydantoin carboxyl-derived carbonyl opens the ring to N-carbamoylvaline.", "reasoning": "Alkaline hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but substantial severity can be required without enzymes. The unusually strong alkalinity and preservation of the product are explicit geochemical bottlenecks. These conditions are not inferred merely from the presence of a hydrothermal environment."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolytic removal of the carbamoyl group", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton from chloride-rich acidic geothermal fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400-435 K", "pressure": "20-60 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 0.5-1.0; proposed residence 12-72 hours. First cool the alkaline effluent, then mix it with a separate acidic fluid and reheat in a downstream pore zone. Cool the product promptly after treatment."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the ureido group followed by carbamic-acid decomposition releases the amino group as valine, together with ammonia and CO2.", "reasoning": "The net transformation is chemically valid, but uncatalyzed biological-style decarbamoylation cannot be assumed. Acid-promoted hydantoin reclosure and destruction during prolonged heating compete with useful release. This is a key condition-dependent bottleneck, and the hydantoin pathways remain conditional until isolated valine recovery is demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Reversible formation of alpha-iminoisovaleric acid", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 6.5-8.0; proposed total ammonia/ammonium 0.05-0.5 M and keto acid 1-20 mM. Use the same flowing compartment as rxn_013 over a proposed 1-48 hour contact period; do not isolate the imino acid."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the keto group and the carbinolamine reversibly loses water, producing a low-abundance imino-acid intermediate.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional imine-forming component of reductive amination. The principal limitation is equilibrium abundance in water, especially when ammonia is mostly protonated. Coupled reduction must trap the intermediate faster than it hydrolyzes. The keto acid is a stated feedstock, not the product of an unsupported hydroxy-acid oxidation."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Stoichiometric native-iron reduction of the imino acid", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_019", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_021"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fresh native-iron surface; mol_019 is a consumed reductant, not a catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 6.5-8.0; chloride-bearing, sulfide-poor and carbonate-poor water; proposed contact 1-48 hours with freshly exposed iron grains. Maintain acidity through inflow. No imposed H2 activity is needed because metallic iron supplies the electrons."}, "mechanism": "Surface electron transfer and protonation reduce the C=N group. Per mole of imino acid, one Fe atom and two protons are consumed, producing one valine and one Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Native iron supplies an explicit two-electron budget and avoids invoking an unidentified hydrogenation catalyst. This is a plausible reductive-amination implementation, not a guaranteed substrate-specific yield. Iron passivation, competing hydrogen evolution, reduction of unaminated keto acid, and limited imine availability remain measurable limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Formation of alpha-ketoisovaleric acid oxime", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Weak aqueous acid for general-acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton at mildly acidic pH", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 4.5-6.5; proposed keto acid 1-20 mM, hydroxylamine near stoichiometric or modestly in excess, and residence 1-48 hours. Keep the shaded formation pool free of metallic iron until condensation has occurred."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl and loses water to form an oxime; E/Z isomers may coexist.", "reasoning": "Oxime formation is well-established carbonyl chemistry and can provide a more persistent nitrogen-bearing intermediate than the ammonia-derived imine. Hydroxylamine availability is a boundary assumption, not an internally demonstrated prebiotic synthesis. Photolysis, metal-induced hydroxylamine destruction, and oxime stability require control."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Four-electron native-iron reduction of the oxime", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001", "mol_021"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fresh native-iron surface; metallic iron is consumed stoichiometrically"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-385 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 3.0-5.0 in chloride-bearing, sulfide-poor water; proposed residence 1-48 hours. Acidic inflow sustains proton supply. Screen at least the stoichiometric iron requirement and monitor dissolved Fe2+, gas evolution, and carbon-containing side products."}, "mechanism": "N-O bond reduction and reduction of the imine functionality furnish the amino acid. Per mole of oxime, two Fe atoms and four protons are consumed, producing one valine, one water, and two Fe2+ ions.", "reasoning": "The reaction has an explicit four-electron and charge-balanced reduction budget. Oxime-to-amine reduction by metallic reductants is established as a reaction class, but selective retention of this alpha-carboxyl group under the proposed iron-water conditions is not demonstrated here. Decarboxylation, incomplete reduction, and iron passivation make this a conditional alternative."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Iron(II)-promoted transamination from alanine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_023"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ coordination promoter, chemically identical to mol_021"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous Fe2+; catalytic turnover is not assumed without measurement", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 6.0-8.0; proposed Fe2+ 1-20 mM, keto acid 1-10 mM, and alanine 0.05-0.5 M; contact 1-7 days. Use naturally Fe(II)-bearing, sulfide-poor water with little phosphate or carbonate. No hydroxylamine, H2, or metallic iron is required."}, "mechanism": "Metal-assisted Schiff-base formation, proton transfer between donor and acceptor carbon skeletons, and hydrolysis transfer the amino group from alanine to the branched keto acid, releasing pyruvate.", "reasoning": "This is a redox-neutral alternative with a different nitrogen-transfer mechanism. Metal-assisted nonenzymatic transamination is chemically credible, but useful valine production from this specific donor-acceptor pair under the proposed conditions is not established here. Branched-substrate kinetics, reversibility, metal complexation, and competing keto-acid reactions must be tested."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Aspartate transamination coupled to oxaloacetate decarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_024"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_023", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Dissolved Fe2+ as a coordination and decarboxylation promoter"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous Fe2+; productive turnover must be measured", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reference pH 6.0-7.5; proposed Fe2+ 1-20 mM, keto acid 1-10 mM, and aspartate 10-100 mM; contact 1-7 days. Use sulfide-poor Fe(II)-bearing water. Subsequent advective dilution of inorganic carbon can favor the coupled net reaction."}, "mechanism": "Aspartate transfers its amino group through a metal-assisted Schiff-base sequence, yielding valine and transient oxaloacetate. Beta-keto-acid decarboxylation converts oxaloacetate to pyruvate and CO2. This node represents the coupled net transformation, not a single elementary reaction.", "reasoning": "Oxaloacetate decarboxylation provides a chemically motivated sink for the donor-derived keto acid and distinguishes this route from simple alanine transamination. The coupled valine-forming implementation is speculative: favorable coproduct decomposition does not establish a useful rate for the preceding transamination. Direct aspartate decomposition and branched-keto-acid side reactions must be quantified."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "na