m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine (racemic)\nOVERALL STRATEGY: A conditional, anoxic network using supplied 2-methylpropanal, hydrogen cyanide, ammonia, carbon dioxide and water. The aldehyde is an explicit boundary feedstock: its geochemical synthesis is not claimed here. Two entry histories, imine capture and cyanohydrin-reservoir processing, converge on the valine aminonitrile. Five subsequent processing histories give ten distinct reaction sequences: acidic hydrolysis, alkaline hydrolysis, acid-to-base staged hydrolysis, base-to-acid staged hydrolysis, and hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. These are ten overlapping, condition-resolved graph pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic synthesis mechanisms. Explicit protonation-state nodes distinguish the acid/base histories. No selective aldehyde hydrogenation, alcohol or hydroxy-acid dehydrogenation, thioamide processing, metal-mediated reduction, or transamination is assumed. All numerical conditions and residence times are proposed screening windows rather than measured substrate-specific rates or yields. The principal unresolved requirements are free-cyanide availability despite Fe(II) complexation, sufficient acid/base inventories, intermediate survival during transport, selective partial hydrolysis for the two pH-switch histories, and sufficiently rapid hydantoin processing. Surface episodes occur in UV-shielded, water-filled sediment pores beneath an otherwise strongly irradiated surface. Hydrothermal episodes occur in separate confined fractures; transport recoveries must be measured rather than assumed.\n\nMOLECULES (19 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C4H8O (Isobutyraldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H3N (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (Water) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C4H9N (Isobutyraldimine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C5H9NO (Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C5H10N2 (Valine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: H+ (Aqueous proton equivalent) [starting]\n  - mol_009: HO- (Hydroxide ion) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C5H12N2O (Valinamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H13N2O+ (Protonated valinamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H12NO2+ (Valinium) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H10NO2- (Valinate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C5H11NO2 (Valine, racemic) [target]\n  - mol_015: H4N+ (Ammonium) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_017: C6H10N2O2 (5-Isopropylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C6H11N2O3- (N-Carbamoylvalinate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C6H12N2O3 (N-Carbamoylvaline) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (16 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_005, mol_004 (Reversible ammonia addition and aldimine formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_007 (Cyanide capture of the aldimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_006 (Reversible aldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_006, mol_002 -> mol_007, mol_004 (Ammonia-driven processing of the cyanohydrin reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_007, mol_004, mol_008 -> mol_011 (Acid-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to protonated valinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_004 -> mol_010 (Base-catalyzed aminonitrile